  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Light southerly flow will steadily increase\ntoday ahead of an approaching cold front. This combined with \nwarm low-level thicknesses will support one more day of above \nnormal temperatures, with highs in the 70s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A potent surface low is forecast to translate east \nfrom the Ohio Valley to the Northern Mid-Atlantic today and \nFriday. Meanwhile, an associated strong cold front is forecast \nto cross ENC tonight. A secondary cold front is then forecast \nto move through late Friday. Moderate to strong CAA behind each \nfront will lead to a fairly drastic drop in temperatures \ncompared to the above normal temperatures will are currently \nexperiencing. Guidance suggests multiple days of highs in the \n50s, with lows in the 30s and 40s. Normal highs this time of \nyear are in the mid to upper 60s, and normal lows are in the 40s\nand 50s. The incoming airmass will be running a solid 10+ \ndegrees below those normal values. Probabilistic guidance shows \nthe highest risk of sub-freezing temperatures coming Saturday \nnight into Sunday morning, although this risk could extend to \nother nights as well. Closer to the coast, some areas away from \nmarine influences could see frost conditions if/where winds \ndecouple enough. Assuming guidance doesn't change, frost/freeze \nheadlines will likely be needed for a large portion of the area \nthis weekend and maybe into early next week.\n\nIn addition to the cold, each front will be accompanied by a period \nof gusty winds of 20-30 mph. This will add to the chill, with wind \nchill values inland making it feel more like the 20s each night from \nFriday through Sunday. The winds may lead to elevated soundside\nwaters, but winds do not currently look to be strong enough to\ncause significant impacts. Perhaps the greatest risk will be\nareas around Roanoke Island that can see some impacts in\nwesterly flow. This risk would be focused in the tonight \nthrough Friday period.",
